#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:6
msgid "Token Munchers"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:8
msgid ""
"An **Incremental TT Muncher** is a recursive macro pattern that processes "
"input token streams by \"eating\" (matching) one or more tokens off the "
"front of the stream, and recursively passing the remainder back to itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:13
msgid "// Copyright 2026 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:14
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:17
msgid "// Base case: Only one method call remains\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:28
msgid "\"Hello, World\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:29
msgid "// Expands recursively to: s.len().trailing_zeros()\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:31
msgid "\"Trailing zeros in length: {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:35
msgid ""
"TT munchers can parse complex custom grammars, but on the other hand, they "
"can be difficult to read and debug. For small embedded DSLs, they are "
"probably the right choice, but for more complex parsers other techniques may "
"be more appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:40
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:42
msgid ""
"Because the recursive call passes down the entire rest of the input, while "
"each step only removes a fixed-size portion of it, TT munchers are quadratic "
"in the size of their input. For large inputs, compile times can bloat, and "
"you might exceed the compiler's default macro recursion limit."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:47
msgid ""
"There are also some limits around the compiler's built-in follow-set "
"restrictions (on which fragment specifiers are allowed immediately preceding "
"a trailing `tt`), needed to prevent parser ambiguity. If we follow the "
"pattern of sticking to `tt` matchers on the left, we mostly avoid this "
"limitation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:55
msgid ""
"Explain that the `tt` specifier is key here because it matches any single "
"token tree (including identifiers, literals, or bracketed groups), allowing "
"the recursive call to accept \"anything else\" in `$tail`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:58
msgid ""
"Note how `chain!(s, len, trailing_zeros)` matches the recursive case, "
"expanding to `chain!(s.len(), trailing_zeros)`, which then matches the base "
"case, expanding to `s.len().trailing_zeros()`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:61
msgid ""
"Warn about rule ordering: a repeated `tt` matcher will also match a single "
"`tt`, so rules expecting the latter should come first."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md:63
msgid ""
"Discuss tradeoffs: if a macro is complex enough to require a TT muncher, "
"there is a chance it may be better expressed as a procedural macro."
msgstr ""
